import FmnistVisualization from "../visualizations/fmnist_visualization/components/VisualizationSideBySide.svelte";
import PairDefinitionsVisualization from "../visualizations/pair_definitions_visualization/components/Visualization.svelte";
import HyperparametersVisualization from "../visualizations/hyperparameters_visualization/components/Visualization.svelte";
import MammothUmapVisualization from "../visualizations/mammoth_visualization/components/VisualizationUmap.svelte";
import ToyVisualization from "../visualizations/toy_visualization/components/Visualization.svelte";
import StabilityVisualization from "../visualizations/stability_visualization/components/Visualization.svelte";

import Katex from "../shared/components/Katex.svelte";
import ExtensionsMnistFigure from "../visualizations/extensions_visualization/components/ExtensionsMnistFigure.svelte";
import LocalMapComparisonFigure from "../visualizations/extensions_visualization/components/LocalMapComparisonFigure.svelte";
import RashomonMnistFigure from "../visualizations/extensions_visualization/components/RashomonMnistFigure.svelte";

const tex = {
  loss: "\\mathcal{L} \\;=\\; w_{\\text{NB}}\\!\\!\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\text{NB}}\\!\\!\\frac{\\tilde{d}_{ij}}{10 + \\tilde{d}_{ij}} \\;+\\; w_{\\text{MN}}\\!\\!\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\text{MN}}\\!\\!\\frac{\\tilde{d}_{ij}}{10^{4} + \\tilde{d}_{ij}} \\;+\\; w_{\\text{FP}}\\!\\!\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\text{FP}}\\!\\!\\frac{1}{1 + \\tilde{d}_{ij}}",
  dij:   "\\tilde{d}_{ij}",
  i:     "i",
  j:     "j",
  wNB:   "w_{\\text{NB}}",
  wMN:   "w_{\\text{MN}}",
  wFP:   "w_{\\text{FP}}",
  wMN_start: "\\approx\\!1000",
  three: "3",
  wNB2:  "w_{\\text{NB}}\\!=\\!2",
  wFP1:  "w_{\\text{FP}}\\!=\\!1",
  wMN3:  "w_{\\text{MN}}\\!=\\!3",
  wNB3:  "w_{\\text{NB}}\\!=\\!3",
  wMN0:  "w_{\\text{MN}}\\!=\\!0",
  wNB1:  "w_{\\text{NB}}\\!=\\!1",
  fpLossInv: "1 / \\text{low-dim distance}",
  fpLoss:  "1 / (\\tilde{d}_{ij} + 1)",
  nnLoss:  "\\tilde{d}_{ij} / (\\tilde{d}_{ij} + 10)",
};
```

<h1 class="article-title">Understanding PaCMAP</h1>

<Spacer height={20} />

Dimensionality reduction (DR) is extremely useful for exploratory data analysis, providing a bird's eye view of high-dimensional data. A high-quality DR method should preserve both *local* structure (i.e., neighborhoods) as well as *global* structure (e.g., placement of clusters). The two most widely used methods for visualisation, [t-SNE](https://lvdmaaten.github.io/tsne/) and [UMAP](https://github.com/lmcinnes/umap), both preserve local structure, but suffer in their ability to preserve global structure. t-SNE tends to produce many small clusters even if there are larger clusters in the data. Its results are inconsistent — the same data run twice with different seeds ends up looking like two different pictures. UMAP inherits its global layout from a spectral or PCA initialisation, so as soon as the initialisation changes, the embedding does too.

[PaCMAP](https://github.com/YingfanWang/PaCMAP) (Pairwise Controlled Manifold Approximation) is a newer method by Wang, Huang, Sun and Rudin (JMLR 2021) that is designed to preserve both local and global structure. Instead of relying on initialisation for global structure, PaCMAP preserves it directly via *mid-near pairs*. PaCMAP's embeddings are fast, stable across runs, and are typically closer to the true global geometry than either t-SNE or UMAP. This blog post discusses more about how it works.

## The mammoth, four ways

Below is a 3D scan of a woolly mammoth (10k points, from the Smithsonian Institute) shown in 3D on the top-left, then projected to 2D by t-SNE, UMAP and PaCMAP. Every panel is fully interactive — you can orbit the 3D data and drag the sliders on the 2D projections to change some of the parameters of the different algorithms. (In general, it's not clear how to set the parameters since dimension reduction is unsupervised.)

<Figure>
  <Mammoth2x2Visualization />
  <span slot="caption">
    <span class="figure-number">Figure 1: </span>The woolly mammoth reduced to 2D by three methods. Top-left is the original 3D data. t-SNE finds the local structure but scrambles the animal's overall shape at any perplexity; UMAP recovers a mammoth-like silhouette when its hyperparameters cooperate but breaks apart at extremes; PaCMAP keeps the mammoth intact across the whole slider range.
  </span>
</Figure>

You'll see that t-SNE and UMAP's DR projections aren't good for many parameter values, and they definitely aren't consistent across parameter values. PaCMAP's projections are much more stable. This is because PaCMAP aims to preserve global structure by design whereas t-SNE and UMAP do not. To see how this works, we need to look at how PaCMAP samples pairs of points, which we will do after we give another example.

## PaCMAP vs. t-SNE, on real high-dimensional data

Below is a comparison of PaCMAP and t-SNE, projecting the 784-dimensional Fashion-MNIST dataset down to 3D. Both algorithms preserve local structure (that is, each class forms its own cluster), but PaCMAP places semantically related categories closer together — the footwear cluster (<FmnistLabel label="sandal"/>, <FmnistLabel label="sneaker"/>, <FmnistLabel label="ankle boot"/>) stays contiguous, and upper-body clothing (<FmnistLabel label="t-shirt/top"/>, <FmnistLabel label="pullover"/>, <FmnistLabel label="coat"/>, <FmnistLabel label="shirt"/>) forms a nearby continent. Again, this is because PaCMAP preserves global structure, whereas t-SNE does not.

<Figure>
  <FmnistVisualization />
  <span slot="caption">
    <span class="figure-number">Figure 2: </span>PaCMAP vs. t-SNE on 2000 Fashion-MNIST images (784 dimensions → 3). Rotate either panel; use the sidebar to focus a category.
  </span>
</Figure>

## How PaCMAP works

PaCMAP constructs three sets of *pairs* of points that are sampled at the start of the algorithm. Every optimization step moves the embedding to position the pairs correctly relative to each other. The three pair types are the entire vocabulary of PaCMAP — everything else in the algorithm depends on how they are drawn and how much we push or pull them apart. The three types are:

**Neighbor pairs (NN).** For each point, we take its `n_neighbors` nearest neighbors in the high-dimensional input space according to a locally scaled Euclidean distance that accounts for local density. These are the *attractive* pairs. Preserving these as neighbors allows DR algorithms to preserve local structure.

**Mid-near pairs (MN).** For each point, we sample 6 random other points, keeping the second closest. We repeat this `MN_ratio * n_neighbors` times per point. These points are unique to PaCMAP. It exerts a weak *attractive* force on these points, which counterbalances the repulsive force on the further pairs (that we'll explain next). **The mid-near pairs are the key ingredient** — they are what make PaCMAP different from every other force-directed embedding algorithm, and they are how PaCMAP preserves global structure.

**Further pairs (FP).** For each point, we sample `FP_ratio * n_neighbors` random other points. These are *repulsive* — the loss pushes them apart. They keep unrelated clusters apart.

The interactive figure below draws all three sets around a highlighted anchor point. Click any point to move the anchor.

<Figure>
  <PairDefinitionsVisualization />
  <span slot="caption">
    <span class="figure-number">Figure 3: </span>The three PaCMAP pair types visualised on a 3D Swiss-roll manifold. Toggle to switch between NN / MN / FP pair sets; drag to rotate; click any point to move the anchor. Pair distances are measured in the raw 3D coordinates (not on screen), so "neighbor" means neighbor along the manifold. Notice that mid-near partners tend to sit along the roll — this is the moderate-distance structure PaCMAP is trying to preserve when it embeds down to 2D.
  </span>
</Figure>

## The PaCMAP loss

PaCMAP is much simpler than t-SNE or UMAP. Its objective function is easy to understand because it has only three terms, one each for NN, MN, and FP.

<Katex formula={tex.loss} />

To give some intuition about it, consider a FP pair, which are two points that are far from each other in the high-dimensional space. We want to *push* these points apart in the low-dimensional embedding, so we assign the FP pair a loss that is similar to <Katex formula={tex.fpLossInv} display={false} />; that way, if the points are far from each other in low dimensions, the loss will be close to 0. (In reality, the loss is <Katex formula={tex.fpLoss} display={false} /> where <Katex formula={tex.dij} display={false} /> is the squared low-dimensional distance, which has a similar effect.) Analogously, for the NN points, we want to *attract* them to each other in the embedding. Their loss is <Katex formula={tex.nnLoss} display={false} />, where 10 is just a small number. This way, if the NN pair is close in the embedding, so their distance is close to 0, the loss is also close to 0. The value of 10 doesn't matter that much — the result doesn't change much if we change it. If the distance is large, then their loss is close to 1. The MN pairs also have an attractive force between them, but it is much smaller because the value in the denominator is 10&thinsp;000, which is several orders of magnitude larger than 10.

These three terms are PaCMAP's entire loss function. The weighting between the terms changes over iterations, where it focuses first on global structure by increasing the weight on the mid-near points, and then later focuses on refining the local structure. And that's really all there is to it — it's a pretty easy algorithm to understand.

## PaCMAP parameters

Once you have read the loss, the three hyperparameters follow immediately:

- `n_neighbors` — how many neighbor pairs each point gets.
- `MN_ratio` — how many mid-near pairs each point gets, as a fraction of `n_neighbors`.
- `FP_ratio` — how many further pairs each point gets, as a fraction of `n_neighbors`.

The defaults (`n_neighbors=10`, `MN_ratio=0.5`, `FP_ratio=2.0`) work on most datasets — as the [Duke DR Group](https://sites.duke.edu/dimensionreduction/) puts it, PaCMAP "has no parameters that are designed to be tuned." That is the ideal for exploratory work: you cannot know what the right hyperparameters are for a dataset you have not yet visualised.

The figure below is the same mammoth as Figure 1, but with all three PaCMAP hyperparameters exposed as sliders so you can see for yourself what each knob does.

<Figure>
  <MammothUmapVisualization />
  <span slot="caption">
    <span class="figure-number">Figure 4: </span>
    Mammoth PaCMAP with all three hyperparameters (`n_neighbors`, `MN_ratio`, `FP_ratio`) exposed. As `n_neighbors` grows, the mammoth's neighbourhoods stitch together into a coherent silhouette; as `MN_ratio` grows, the mid-near pull tightens the mammoth's outline; `FP_ratio` sets the amount of repulsive breathing room between limbs. At very low `n_neighbors` the mammoth loses its head — this is the local-structure regime where PaCMAP cannot see far enough to reconnect the skull.
  </span>
</Figure>

The same effect on toy shapes and interactively across a grid:

<Figure>
  <ToyVisualization />
  <span slot="caption">
    <span class="figure-number">Figure 5: </span>Interactive PaCMAP animations on 24 toy datasets. Hit ▶ to watch the optimisation animate through six snapshots taken during the three-phase schedule; sliders snap to the nearest precomputed triplet.
  </span>
</Figure>

<Figure>
  <HyperparametersVisualization />
  <span slot="caption">
    <span class="figure-number">Figure 6: </span>PaCMAP projections of various toy datasets across a grid of `n_neighbors` and `MN_ratio` values.
  </span>
</Figure>

## Random seed initialisation

We hinted at this in the opening: t-SNE and UMAP embeddings shatter based on initialisation, while PaCMAP does not. The figure below makes it a direct comparison. Pick a dataset, then drag the seed slider — every embedding for seed ≥ 2 is Procrustes-aligned to the seed-1 embedding, so what you see is not rigid rotation, it is real seed variance.

<Figure>
  <StabilityVisualization />
  <span slot="caption">
    <span class="figure-number">Figure 7: </span>Method comparison across 8 random seeds on two canonical benchmarks: the 3D mammoth (a global-structure test) and MNIST at 784 dimensions (a local-structure test). UMAP and t-SNE reorganise their embeddings dramatically from seed to seed; PaCMAP stays visually stable.
  </span>
</Figure>

On the mammoth (a hard global-structure benchmark), PaCMAP is clearly the most stable of the three; on MNIST — whose classes separate so cleanly that even t-SNE converges to the same layout across seeds — PaCMAP is within noise of the others. The pattern generalises: the more the dataset relies on global geometry, the more the stability gap between PaCMAP and t-SNE / UMAP widens.

## Extensions of PaCMAP

There are currently three important follow-up algorithms to PaCMAP. Each is shown on the same 2000-image MNIST subset used earlier.

### LocalMAP — dynamic local graph adjustment

**LocalMAP** (Wang, Huang, Sun & Rudin, *Pairwise Controlled Manifold Approximation with Local Adjusted Graph*, [AAAI 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.15426)) addresses PaCMAP's one visible weakness: neighbor pairs are drawn *once* at the start of optimization and never revisited. If that initial neighbor graph is noisy or ambiguous (which it might be since the neighbors are determined from Euclidean distance), PaCMAP inherits its mistakes. LocalMAP dynamically re-adjusts the neighbor graph during the final phase of optimization, tightening clusters that were originally sampled with poor neighbors. In practice, the effect is that clusters separate better without giving up PaCMAP's global structure preservation or stability. LocalMAP ships inside the `pacmap` Python package as `pacmap.LocalMAP` — a drop-in replacement.

<Figure>
  <ExtensionsMnistFigure method="localmap" />
  <span slot="caption">
    <span class="figure-number">Figure 8: </span>LocalMAP on the full 70 000-image MNIST dataset (784 → 2). Compare to the PaCMAP MNIST in Figure 7 — cluster boundaries are noticeably crisper.
  </span>
</Figure>

### ParamRepulsor — parametric PaCMAP with stronger repulsion

**ParamRepulsor** (Huang, Wang & Rudin, *Navigating the Effect of Parametrization for Dimensionality Reduction*, [NeurIPS 2024](https://openreview.net/pdf?id=eYNYnYle41)) makes PaCMAP *parametric*: instead of moving points in the low-dimensional space to optimize an objective, it trains a small neural network that maps any high-dimensional point to its low-dimensional coordinate. Once trained, the network embeds new points in constant time — no re-fitting required. The paper's key finding is that naïvely parametrising a PaCMAP-style loss does not provide enough repulsion, so ParamRepulsor introduces a stronger repulsion term (the "repulsor") that restores cluster separation. The Python package is `parampacmap`; installation is [documented in the repo](https://github.com/hyhuang00/ParamRepulsor).

<Figure>
  <ExtensionsMnistFigure method="parampacmap" />
  <span slot="caption">
    <span class="figure-number">Figure 9: </span>ParamRepulsor (parametric PaCMAP with strengthened repulsion) on the same 2000-image MNIST subset.
  </span>
</Figure>

### RashomonDR — the Rashomon set of DR embeddings

**RashomonDR** (Sun, Huang, Parikh & Rudin, *The Rashomon Effect for Visualizing High-Dimensional Data*, [AISTATS 2026](https://github.com/williamsyy/RashomonDR)) starts from an uncomfortable fact: **dimensionality reduction is inherently non-unique.** For any dataset there is not one "correct" embedding but a whole set of embeddings — the *Rashomon set* — that all preserve the high-dimensional structure equally well while looking visually different. A single embedding hides the ambiguity in the data. RashomonDR formalises the Rashomon set for DR. Within the Rashomon set, it shows how to optimize secondary objectives, such as aligning embeddings to a PCA-informed reference frame, or aligning the axes with known concepts. An important contribution of this paper is to extract the *common knowledge* that survives across the embeddings in the Rashomon set and preserve it. The figure below shows three embeddings from the Rashomon set on MNIST — all cluster the classes correctly, but the *arrangement* of the classes changes.

<Figure>
  <RashomonMnistFigure />
  <span slot="caption">
    <span class="figure-number">Figure 10: </span>Three members of the Rashomon set for MNIST — each is a valid PaCMAP embedding under a different random-init seed. All three preserve local structure (classes still cluster correctly), yet the arrangement of the classes across the plane differs from one member to the next. That layout disagreement is the Rashomon variability the paper is measuring.
  </span>
</Figure>

The RashomonDR pipeline turns that variability into a single, more trustworthy embedding by extracting what all valid members agree on — the *Common Knowledge PaCMAP* (CK-PaCMAP) embedding on the right.

<Figure>
  <img src="rashomon_ck_comparison.png" alt="PaCMAP vs. Common Knowledge PaCMAP on MNIST" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; margin: 0 auto;" />
  <span slot="caption">
    <span class="figure-number">Figure 11: </span>PaCMAP (left) vs. Common Knowledge PaCMAP (right) on the full 70&thinsp;000-image MNIST dataset, produced with the reference RashomonDR pipeline. Both share the same neighbor-pair graph (thin gray edges overlay the scatter). CK-PaCMAP aggregates multiple valid Rashomon members into a single embedding that keeps only the structure every member agrees on — the classes that survive aggregation are the ones that are stably preserved across the whole Rashomon set.
  </span>
</Figure>

## Conclusion

PaCMAP is a strong dimensionality reduction algorithm for exploratory data analysis. It's fast, robust, and preserves global structure by design. If you are already using t-SNE or UMAP for visualisation, PaCMAP is worth trying if you want to preserve the geometry of the high-dimensional data.

